{"abstract":"Transfer learning has become an increasingly popular approach in medical imaging, as it offers a solution to the challenge of training models with limited dataset sizes. The ability to leverage knowledge from pre-trained models has proven to be beneficial in various medical imaging applications, such as disease diagnosis, classification of pathological conditions, and early detection of abnormalities in imaging modalities like X-rays, MRIs, and CT scans. Pre-training on ImageNet, a dataset originally designed for natural image classification, has become the standard in the field. However, unlike natural images, which typically feature distinct global objects, medical images often rely on subtle local texture variations to indicate pathology. These stark differences between natural and medical images have spurred exploration into alternative pre-training strategies, such as using existing medical datasets, modifying them, or developing new datasets specifically designed for medical imaging. However, how to effectively choose between various alternatives and what impact the choice of source dataset has on the model’s final representations remain unclear. This thesis examines the mechanics of transfer learning in medical image classification and explores the broader impact of the source dataset, extending beyond transfer performance. The aim is to provide insights and tools to guide the selection of appropriate source datasets for medical image classification. First, we compare learned intermediate representations of the models pre-trained on natural and medical source datasets. Our results indicate that, while models achieve comparable performance, they converge to distinct representations, which further diverge after fine-tuning. Next, we investigate the impact of these different representations on model generalization by fine-tuning models on targets curated to include systematically controlled confounders. The results show substantial differences in robustness to shortcut learning between models pre-trained on natural images and those pre-trained on medical images, despite similar classification performance. Finally, we benchmark existing transferability metrics for source dataset selection and show that current metrics–designed and validated on natural image datasets–perform poorly in the context of medical image classification. This highlights the need for transferability metrics specifically tailored to medical imaging tasks. To address this, we propose a novel transferability metric that integrates feature quality with gradient information, overcoming the self-source bias inherent in previous methods that rely solely on feature quality. Our results show that this approach outperforms existing metrics in source dataset selection for medical image classification.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Juodelyte, Dovile"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808918"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"from cats to cts cross domain transfer in medical image classification","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109527091/Dovile_Thesis.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808918","ids":["itu-989755ae-5afc-4bf0-bd49-9657a745fb2b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-EAAFBFCA4BF52DB5B3A1C4543E0C4079"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109527091/Dovile_Thesis.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Pattern Recognition Revisited lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dovile","key":"juodelytepau","last":"Juodelyte","locid":"6800c64b-0e54-49cb-a703-384b4987b70c","name":"Juodelyte, Dovile","orcid":"0000-0002-6195-1120","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779495326"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"234","pages":"106","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-989755ae-5afc-4bf0-bd49-9657a745fb2b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"From Cats to CTs: Cross-Domain Transfer in Medical Image Classification","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808918","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The advancement of machine learning algorithms in medical image analysis requires the expansion of training datasets. A popular and cost-effective approach is automated annotation extraction from free-text medical reports, primarily due to the high costs associated with expert clinicians annotating medical images, such as chest X-rays. However, it has been shown that the resulting datasets are susceptible to biases and shortcuts. Another strategy to increase the size of a dataset is crowdsourcing, a widely adopted practice in general computer vision with some success in medical image analysis. In a similar vein to crowdsourcing, we enhance two publicly available chest X-ray datasets by incorporating non-expert annotations. However, instead of using diagnostic labels, we annotate shortcuts in the form of tubes. We collect 3.5k chest drain annotations for NIH-CXR14, and 1k annotations for four different tube types in PadChest, and create the Non-Expert Annotations of Tubes in X-rays (NEATX) dataset. We train a chest drain detector with the non-expert annotations that generalizes well to expert labels. Moreover, we compare our annotations to those provided by experts and show “moderate” to “almost perfect” agreement. Finally, we present a pathology agreement study to raise awareness about the quality of ground truth annotations. We make our dataset available on Zenodo at https://zenodo.org/records/14944064 and our code available at https://github.com/purrlab/chestxr-label-reliability.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Cheplygina, Veronika","Damgaard, Cathrine","Eriksen, Trine Naja","Juodelyte, Dovile","Jiménez Sánchez, Amelia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828154"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Annotation","Chest X ray","Dataset","Medical imaging"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"augmenting chest x ray datasets with non expert annotations","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105011823170","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828154","identifier":[{"id":"105011823170","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5b84e052-a0e6-46bc-a17c-98c87b9d74ba"],"keyword":["annotation","chest x-ray","dataset","medical imaging","research artifact"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-3F7412EFFC5DDB13706E739BC7A0C0F8"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Pattern Recognition Revisited lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Veronika","key":"cheplyginapau","last":"Cheplygina","locid":"1c4bfd79-4336-4b17-a672-9acb465f15c0","name":"Cheplygina, Veronika","orcid":"0000-0003-0176-9324","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Cathrine","key":"damgaardpau","last":"Damgaard","name":"Damgaard, Cathrine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine Naja","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","name":"Eriksen, Trine Naja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dovile","key":"juodelytepau","last":"Juodelyte","locid":"6800c64b-0e54-49cb-a703-384b4987b70c","name":"Juodelyte, Dovile","orcid":"0000-0002-6195-1120","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amelia","key":"jiménezsánchezpau","last":"Jiménez-Sánchez","locid":"64730011-f493-4dbb-abf6-b48924ddc794","name":"Jiménez Sánchez, Amelia","name_org":"Jiménez-Sánchez, Amelia","orcid":"0000-0001-7870-0603","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-98688-8_10","eisbn":["9783031986888"],"isbn":["9783031986871"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"133-144","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Medical Image Understanding and Analysis"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105011823170"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5b84e052-a0e6-46bc-a17c-98c87b9d74ba"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Augmenting Chest X-ray Datasets with Non-Expert Annotations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828154","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Datasets play a critical role in medical imaging research, yet issues such as label quality, shortcuts, and metadata are often overlooked. This lack of attention may harm the generalizability of algorithms and, consequently, negatively impact patient outcomes. While existing medical imaging literature reviews mostly focus on machine learning (ML) methods, with only a few focusing on datasets for specific applications, these reviews remain static-they are published once and not updated thereafter. This fails to account for emerging evidence, such as biases, shortcuts, and additional annotations that other researchers may contribute after the dataset is published. We refer to these newly discovered findings of datasets as research artifacts. To address this gap, we propose a living review that continuously tracks public datasets and their associated research artifacts across multiple medical imaging applications. Our approach includes a framework for the living review to monitor data documentation artifacts, and an SQL database to visualize the citation relationships between research artifact and dataset. Lastly, we discuss key considerations for creating medical imaging datasets, review best practices for data annotation, discuss the significance of shortcuts and demographic diversity, and emphasize the importance of managing datasets throughout their entire lifecycle. Our demo is publicly available at http://inthepicture.itu.dk/.","author_count":29,"contributors":["Jiménez-Sánchez, Amelia","Avlona, Natalia Rozalia","De Boer, Sarah","Campello, Víctor M.","Feragen, Aasa","Ferrante, Enzo","Ganz, Melanie","Gichoya, Judy Wawira","Gonzalez, Camila","Groefsema, Steff","Hering, Alessa","Hulman, Adam","Joskowicz, Leo","Juodelyte, Dovile","Kandemir, Melih","Kooi, Thijs","Lérida, Jorge Del Pozo","Li, Livie Yumeng","Pacheco, Andre","Rädsch, Tim","Reyes, Mauricio","Sourget, Théo","Van Ginneken, Bram","Wen, David","Weng, Nina","Xu, Jack Junchi","Zajaç, Hubert Dariusz","Zuluaga, Maria A.","Cheplygina, Veronika"],"cuid":["S03-088.78-822814"],"event":{"end":"2025-06-26","place":"Athens, gr","start":"2025-06-23","title":"8th Annual ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency, FAccT 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, Central","America, North","America, South","Asia, East","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jiménez-Sánchez, Amelia"],"country":["Argentina","Brazil","Dominican Republic","France","Germany","Israel","Netherlands","South Korea","Spain","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Consejo Nacional de Investigaciones Científicas y Técnicas","EURECOM","Emory University","Federal University of Espírito Santo","German Cancer Research Center","Hebrew University of Jerusalem","Heidelberg University ","Ibero-American University","Lunit, Inc.","National Scientific and Technical Research Council","Oxford University Hospital","Oxford University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust","Radboud Universiteit Nijmegen","Radboud University Nijmegen","Stanford University","UB","Universidade Federal do Espírito Santo","Universitat de Barcelona","University of Barcelona","University of Bern","University of Buenos Aires","University of Groningen"],"keyword":["Bias","Data governance","Healthcare","Living review","Medical imaging","Open data","Research artifacts","Shortcuts"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"OT":["Radiological AI testcenter"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"in the picture medical imaging datasets artifacts and their living review","src":["dtu","itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3715275.3732035","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project has received funding from the Independent Research Council Denmark (DFF) Inge Lehmann 1134-00017B. The “In the Picture: Medical Imaging Datasets” workshop was funded by DFF and the Danish Data Science Academy (DDSA) with Grant ID 2024-2342. We extend our gratitude to the speakers and participants of the “Datasets through the Looking-Glass” webinar, who have helped to shape this research. EF gratefully acknowledges the support of the Google Award for Inclusion Research (AIR) Program. AJS was financed by the DFF grant MMC. AH is employed at Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus that is partly funded by a donation from the Novo Nordisk Foundation. AH is supported by a Data Science Emerging Investigator grant by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF22OC0076725). TR was supported by a scholarship from the Hanns Seidel Foundation with funds from the Federal Ministry of Education and Research Germany (BMBF). DW received funding from the Jill and Herbert Hunt Scholarship, University of Oxford. MAZ is funded by TRAIN (ANR-22-FAI1-0003-02). We thank Freepick for the icons in Fig. 2."],"id":"S03-088.78-822814","identifier":[{"id":"105010824939","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-17300733-27d0-4a80-9463-bb7804f00f5b","itu-fa7d9628-df39-4c9c-a3cb-12c4d097b590","ku-c7de5a91-821f-4f98-9538-a2ede6e17660"],"keyword":["Bias","Data governance","Healthcare","Living review","Medical imaging","Open data","Research artifacts","Shortcuts"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-FC1A61479BBB48B76657A4ECDE87748B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"l